WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.783
RTIC helps structure access to shared
resources,

2
00:00:02.863 --> 00:00:06.287
but shared state still deserves careful
security and reliability review.

3
00:00:06.367 --> 00:00:08.650
A shared resource should exist for a clear
reason.

4
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:09.926
If many tasks need access,

5
00:00:10.006 --> 00:00:13.919
reviewers should understand why the state
is shared and whether a narrower ownership

6
00:00:13.999 --> 00:00:15.086
model would be clearer.

7
00:00:15.166 --> 00:00:16.470
Lock scope should be narrow.

8
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:20.518
Exclusive access should protect the
smallest operation that truly needs protection.

9
00:00:20.598 --> 00:00:23.805
If a critical section grows to include
parsing, formatting, logging,

10
00:00:23.885 --> 00:00:26.766
communication, storage access, or slow
peripheral activity,

11
00:00:26.846 --> 00:00:29.563
the code may be mixing data protection
with unrelated work.

12
00:00:29.643 --> 00:00:33.230
Synchronization choices affect timing
behavior, not only data correctness.

13
00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:36.354
A lock that is technically correct can
still delay important work,

14
00:00:36.434 --> 00:00:39.913
create unexpected latency, or make
degraded conditions harder to analyze.

15
00:00:39.993 --> 00:00:43.526
Priority and resource access should be
reviewed together so timing impact is

16
00:00:43.606 --> 00:00:44.040
visible.

17
00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:47.545
Defensive firmware avoids hidden shared
mutation and broad global state.

18
00:00:47.625 --> 00:00:50.342
It keeps shared resources close to the
tasks that need them,

19
00:00:50.422 --> 00:00:52.107
documents why sharing is necessary,

20
00:00:52.187 --> 00:00:55.448
and limits the amount of work performed
while exclusive access is held.

21
00:00:55.528 --> 00:00:58.300
Reviewers should ask what invariant the
resource protects,

22
00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.609
which tasks can access it, how long access
lasts,

23
00:01:00.689 --> 00:01:03.515
whether slow operations occur inside the
protected section,

24
00:01:03.595 --> 00:01:06.041
and what happens when a task is
interrupted, delayed,

25
00:01:06.121 --> 00:01:07.860
or unable to complete its normal path.
